    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **21 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization on the owner-creation path in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`.\n\n**What changed** (`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n\n1. **New `normalizeAddress(String)` helper** \u2014 trims, collapses internal whitespace to single spaces, upper-cases, and expands standalone street-type abbreviations per token (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). A `null`/blank input normalizes to the empty string. E.g. `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`.\n\n2. **In `addOwner`**, right after mapping the DTO to an `Owner`:\n   - Compute the normalized address.\n   - If it's empty after normalization, reject with **400 Bad Request** (the required-field check).\n   - Otherwise `owner.setAddress(normalizedAddress)` so the normalized value is stored and returned.\n\nBecause the normalized address is set on the owner *before* the duplicate/household logic runs \u2014 and every owner is created through this same path \u2014 both the household duplicate detection and the shared `householdId` derivation (via `normalizeForHousehold` / `householdId`) now operate on the normalized form, as the spec requires. That's why `\"12 Main St\"`, `\"12  main  street\"`, and `\"12  MAIN  ST\"` all collapse to the same household.\n\nNote: the DTO already carries a `.*\\S.*` pattern that also catches whitespace-only input, so the blank case is defended at both the validation boundary and the explicit post-normalization check.\n\nI did not touch the V2 controller (it only lists owners) or `updateOwner` \u2014 the spec scopes normalization to owner *creation*.",
